    "['delete set protocols [parameter1] [parameter2] [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation from Juniper to HUAWEI for the OSPF network removal commands:\\n\\n##\\nundo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255\\nundo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In HUAWEI devices, \"undo\" is used instead of \"delete\" to remove configurations\\n2. The network command format in HUAWEI OSPF uses wildcard masks (0.0.0.255) instead of prefix lengths (/24)\\n3. The area specification is typically done at the OSPF process level in HUAWEI, not repeated in each network statement\\n4. The \"set\" keyword from Juniper isn\\'t used in HUAWEI configurations', 'trans_template': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'undo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'undo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the translation from Juniper to HUAWEI for the OSPF network removal commands:\\n\\n##\\nundo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255\\nundo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nNotes:\\n1. In HUAWEI devices, \"undo\" is used instead of \"delete\" to remove configurations\\n2. The network command format in HUAWEI OSPF uses wildcard masks (0.0.0.255) instead of prefix lengths (/24)\\n3. The area specification is typically done at the OSPF process level in HUAWEI, not repeated in each network statement\\n4. The \"set\" keyword from Juniper isn\\'t used in HUAWEI configurations', 'trans_template': 'undo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255'}]]": 1,
    "['network [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'parent_command': ['undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255'], 'root': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nundo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei devices, the \"delete\" equivalent is \"undo\"\\n2. OSPF network statements in Huawei use wildcard masks (0.0.0.255 for /24) instead of prefix notation\\n3. The area number format is different (Huawei doesn\\'t require the \"set protocols\" prefix)\\n4. The command structure is simplified in Huawei compared to Juniper\\'s hierarchical configuration', 'trans_template': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255'}]]": 1,
    "['network [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'undo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'parent_command': ['undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255'], 'root': 'undo network 172.16.1.0 0.0.0.255', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nundo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n1. In Huawei OSPF configuration, the \"undo\" command is used to delete configurations (equivalent to Juniper\\'s \"delete\" command)\\n2. The network statement format in Huawei uses wildcard mask (0.0.0.255) instead of prefix length (/24)\\n3. The area number is not specified in this single command as it would be configured in the OSPF area context\\n4. The Juniper \"set protocols ospf area 0 network\" becomes simply \"network\" in Huawei (after entering OSPF area view)', 'trans_template': 'undo network 172.17.1.0 0.0.0.255'}]]": 1,
